a, From left to right: test accuracy on PS-MNIST, SMNIST, SHD and SSC for the proposed DMP-SNN. For all datasets, the white dashed curve denotes an iso-accuracy contour, where each point corresponds to a (memory size, state-buffer length) pair achieving the same target accuracy (chosen to be competitive with previous works). The iso-accuracy contours indicate overall trends. The curve highlights two configurations of DMP-SNN: solution I, a single operating point on the curve, and the most parameter-efficient configuration that matches SOTA performance; solution II (accuracy-parameter trade-off), a higher-capacity configuration with low additional parameter cost. b, Learning curves for baseline and the proposed DMP-SNN on PS-MNIST, S-MNIST, SHD and SSC. The central line indicates the mean accuracy across n = 5 independent runs, and the shaded area represents the standard error of the mean. Under identical training settings and neuron counts, the DMP-SNN starts from a higher accuracy and converges rapidly, consistently outperforming baseline across datasets. c, Comparison of DMP-SNN with recent strong SNN models. DMP-SNN matches or outperforms past work while using a purely feedforward architecture and notably fewer parameters. M, model size. We compared the recent FSNN45, DSNN17, RSNN45, LSNN14, GLIF46, PLIF10, ASRNN13, SRNN72, DH24, TC-LIF73, Rhythm49 and DelRec74.Table 1
